<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ram Mohan Roy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1772">1772</a>–<a href="/years/1833">1833</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Raja Ram Mohan Roy, Rammohun Roy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ram Mohan Roy was a Bengali scholar and social reformer, often called the father of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. He campaigned against sati, the burning of widows, which the British banned in Bengal in 1829, and argued for women's property rights and modern education. In 1828 he founded the Brahmo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabha-libya">Sabha</a>, a monotheistic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> reform society. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bristol">Bristol</a> while visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ram-mohan-roy.jpg" alt="Ram Mohan Roy" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rembrandt Peale · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Raja_Ram_Mohun_Roy,_1833.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
